# F12 at the bridge boundary: does the app's own physical-controller bridge -- connect, per-control
# merge, release, disconnect -- drive the same pad-assembly boundary a finger does, once per port
# frame?
#
# What is synthetic and what is not. A Simulator cannot be handed a physical pad, so the injector is
# the framework's own GCVirtualController: a real GCController in GCController.controllers that
# posts the real connect and disconnect notifications and delivers through the real
# valueChangedHandler. The connect, the sample delivery and the disconnect therefore travel the
# framework's own path into `BallpadPhysicalControllers.mm`'s handler, and only the hand holding the
# pad is scripted. The virtual pad is configured `hidden`, so the framework does not draw its own
# touch surface over the game and the run is not exercising a second visible interface.
#
# Where the sweep lands, stated rather than glossed. The injector is counted in the *port's* frames
# from the frame the app is past launch, so its fourteen steps run over roughly the first 440 port
# frames and the sweep is over while the front end is still drawing its first screen. That is the
# honest reading of what is being measured, and it is enough for this row because the boundary doc
# 34's F12 names is scene-independent: `PortHostUIPollPad` runs once per frame from
# `PortUpdateSyntheticInput` before the VBlank pad pass, in every scene, and `PadStatus::s_Current[0]`
# is the pad the game's own tasks read whenever they read one. What this row does *not* claim is that
# a scripted press reached gameplay -- that reading is F04's, taken inside the port's own match
# bracket, and the two rows are deliberately separate.
#
# How the claims are read. The adapter's sampler prints one `controller:` line per change: what the
# bridge published into the mixer's controller half (`pub`), what the host's per-frame poll made of
# that mixer (`offer`), and the engine's own pad (`engine`). The reduction is
# scripts/native/f12-pad-summary.awk and the clauses are stated in scripts/native/run-scenario.sh.
# Every claim about the stops in between is read out of the same log by that reduction; the single
# `wait-log` below is the sweep's end rather than a second opinion on them. One wait, on the last
# line the script can print, because a wait begins where the log ends when it starts: the bridge
# prints its connect forty milliseconds after its mapping line and its disconnect fifteen
# milliseconds after `done`, and the driver samples the file every fifty, so a second wait would
# begin past the line it was looking for no matter how early it was reached. The end-of-sweep line
# is also the one that cannot be matched early, since nothing else in the log carries it.
env STRIKERS_LOG_CONTROLLER=1
env STRIKERS_FAKE_PAD=controller
wait-log done, 14 steps, disconnecting
require-frame 460
quit
